{
  "summary": "Read-only documentation structure scout findings for `docs/`, root vault entry files, representative plans/reports/related-work/remarks, and broad Markdown patterns. No files were changed and no project commands/tests were run. The vault already has a clear top-level Obsidian entry path (`Home.md` → `docs/README.md`/`remark/README.md`), but navigation friction comes from duplicated roots, missing indexes for high-volume dated/report folders, stale related-work links, and very large spec files sitting beside normal notes.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "Home.md",
      "description": "Top-level Obsidian MOC. Lines 7-10 link to `docs/README`, `agents/README`, `remark/README`, and `docs/research_plan`; lines 12-16 define the vault rules, including wiki links only in navigation pages and relative Markdown links for outside-Obsidian readability."
    },
    {
      "path": "README.md",
      "description": "GitHub-facing root README. Lines 38-39 tell users to open the repository root as the Obsidian vault and start at `Home.md`; lines 18-31 list `docs/`, `agents/`, and `remark/`, duplicating some Home-level navigation in plain Markdown."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/README.md",
      "description": "Primary docs map. Lines 3 and 7-15 make this the human-facing docs MOC with wiki links to research plan, design document, specs, and each related-work topic; lines 17-27 summarize record areas but do not deep-link into report/plan topic indexes."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/AGENTS.md",
      "description": "Authoritative docs placement conventions. Lines 8-14 map plans/reports/specs/related work; lines 17-23 define placement rules; lines 26-29 list anti-patterns. Useful as implementation guidance for any docs reorg plan."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/related_work/README.md",
      "description": "Topic-first related-work index. Lines 13-18 list six topic notes; lines 21-36 cross-link thesis/literature layers, but lines 24-29 and 36 reference old flat `docs/report/00N...` paths that do not exist in the current tree."
    },
    {
      "path": "remark/README.md",
      "description": "Single-point insight MOC. Lines 5-15 link all numbered remark files; line 17 links back to `Home`, making it a good Obsidian entry point with consistent wiki-link style."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/report/AGENTS.md",
      "description": "Report taxonomy and naming rules. Lines 8-15 define topic groups; lines 18-24 require topic-local `NNN.<descriptor>.<ext>` names; lines 27-30 warn not to place new reports directly under `docs/report/`. There is no `docs/report/README.md` index to expose this taxonomy to vault readers."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/plan/2026-06-11/01.customized-integration-bench-report.plan.md",
      "description": "Representative current plan. Uses `# Title`, `## Goal`, `## Scope`, `## Intended changes`, `## Verification`, `## Completion notes`, and records completion evidence. Naming follows `NN.topic.state.md` documented in `docs/README.md` line 19."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/report/benchmarking/055.customized_integration_results_2026-06-11.md",
      "description": "Representative report. Lines 1-35 provide title, at-a-glance summary, data source, generated artifact list, and filtering rule; lines 37, 52, 63, and 65 embed relative PNG images, which are GitHub-friendly and Obsidian-compatible."
    },
    {
      "path": "docs/report/literature/papers/cloud-scale-characterization-of-remote-procedure-calls/README.md",
      "description": "Representative paper-folder README. It uses a stable structured template: paper identity, why it matters, method/evaluation/findings, limits, repo takeaways, and source links. This is a strong Obsidian note pattern for paper-level navigation."
    }
  ],
  "architecture": "Current organization:\n- Vault root is intentionally the repository root, not `docs/`. `Home.md` is the curated Obsidian landing page; `README.md` is the GitHub landing page and points readers to `Home.md`.\n- `docs/` is the human-facing research KB. `docs/README.md` is the docs MOC and links to `docs/research_plan`, `docs/design_document`, `docs/specs/README`, and the six `docs/related_work/*` topic notes.\n- `docs/plan/YYYY-MM-DD/NN.topic.state.md` stores dated human-readable project records. Current samples include plan/complete/draft states. There is no `docs/plan/README.md` and no per-date MOC.\n- `docs/report/<topic>/NNN.<descriptor>.md` stores findings by topic. `docs/report/AGENTS.md` documents the taxonomy, but there is no reader-facing `docs/report/README.md`; users must infer available topics from directory names or the brief list in `docs/README.md`.\n- `docs/report/literature/papers/<slug>/README.md` is a paper-level summary layer with local `paper.md`/PDF artifacts. This is the most Obsidian-friendly deep subtree because each paper folder has an entry README.\n- `docs/related_work/` is topic-first synthesis and has a good `README.md` topic map. `remark/` is a sibling root for short durable observations and has a complete numbered MOC.\n- `docs/specs/` has a small README plus very large spec Markdown files (`dsa_architecture_spec.md` around 622 KB, `iax(iaa)_architecture_spec.md` around 137 KB), which are useful as local source material but heavy as normal vault notes.\n\nIndex/MOC patterns:\n- Strong: `Home.md`, `docs/README.md`, `docs/related_work/README.md`, `docs/specs/README.md`, `remark/README.md`, and per-paper `README.md` files.\n- Weak/missing: `docs/plan/README.md`, date-level plan indexes, `docs/report/README.md`, and report-topic indexes such as `docs/report/benchmarking/README.md`, `docs/report/architecture/README.md`, and `docs/report/literature/README.md`.\n- Reader-facing taxonomy currently lives partly in agent guidance (`docs/report/AGENTS.md`), which Obsidian users may not treat as a navigation page.\n\nLink and metadata conventions observed:\n- Navigation pages intentionally use Obsidian wiki links (`Home.md` lines 7-10, `docs/README.md` lines 7-15, `remark/README.md` lines 5-17).\n- Content notes and reports mostly use normal relative Markdown links and image embeds, preserving GitHub readability. Example: `docs/report/benchmarking/055...` uses relative PNG embeds at lines 37, 52, 63, and 65.\n- Search found no `tags:`, `aliases:`, or YAML metadata fields in the checked scope. The vault is organized by folders, numeric prefixes, filenames, and MOCs rather than frontmatter/tag navigation.\n- Related-work README uses plain code-form paths for its cross-links, not clickable Markdown links, and some are stale.\n\nFriction for Obsidian navigation:\n1. Duplicated entry roots: `README.md`, `Home.md`, and `docs/README.md` all describe how to begin. This is understandable for GitHub + Obsidian dual use, but the distinction should stay explicit: root README for GitHub, Home for vault, docs README for docs MOC.\n2. Missing high-volume indexes: `docs/plan/` and `docs/report/` contain many dated/topic files but lack reader-facing MOCs. This makes Obsidian graph/search usable but manual browsing poor.\n3. Stale related-work links: `docs/related_work/README.md` lines 24-29 point to old flat `docs/report/003...008...` files. Current files are under `docs/report/literature/001...006...`; the old paths were confirmed missing.\n4. Heavy specs: `docs/specs/dsa_architecture_spec.md` and `docs/specs/iax(iaa)_architecture_spec.md` are authoritative but large. They should remain local source material, but navigation should route readers through `docs/specs/README.md` rather than encouraging casual opening.\n5. Report taxonomy not exposed to humans: `docs/report/AGENTS.md` has the best topic-group definitions, but there is no matching `docs/report/README.md` MOC.\n6. Plan/report duplication with `agents/`: root/docs guidance says agent workflow material belongs under `agents/`, while project records belong under `docs/`. The boundary is documented, but similar dated filenames under both trees can confuse Obsidian browsing unless MOCs explain the split.\n\nRecommended plan changes for the implementing agent:\n- Add or update reader-facing MOCs only; do not move the whole vault unless explicitly requested.\n- Create `docs/report/README.md` mirroring the topic taxonomy from `docs/report/AGENTS.md`, with links to key topic folders and recent/high-value reports.\n- Create `docs/plan/README.md` explaining date folders, filename states, and the difference between project records in `docs/plan` and agent execution plans in `agents/plan`.\n- Fix `docs/related_work/README.md` stale report references to current `docs/report/literature/00N...` paths and make them clickable relative Markdown links.\n- Consider lightweight topic indexes for the biggest report folders first (`benchmarking`, `architecture`, `literature`) rather than indexing every report file at once.\n- Keep wiki links limited to MOCs/navigation pages and use relative Markdown links inside content notes, matching the existing vault rule.\n\n### Critical Files for Implementation\n\n- `Home.md` — Vault landing page; changes here affect every Obsidian user’s first navigation step.\n- `docs/README.md` — Main docs MOC; should point to any new plan/report indexes and preserve the GitHub/Obsidian boundary.\n- `docs/related_work/README.md` — Existing topic map with stale report paths that should be corrected.\n- `docs/report/AGENTS.md` — Source of report taxonomy/naming conventions to translate into a reader-facing `docs/report/README.md`.\n- `docs/AGENTS.md` — Source of docs placement rules and `docs`/`agents`/`remark` boundaries to preserve during the reorg."
}
